# Caddy for the production deployment. Two site blocks, because invariant
# 10 is two hostnames: the application and the origin sample bytes are
# served from. They must be different names, and Caddy is where that
# becomes true on the wire.
#
# {$VAR} is Caddy's own environment placeholder, resolved when the config
# loads. All three come from infra/production/caddy.env by way of the
# caddy service's env_file, and that file holds those three and nothing
# else: this is the one process the internet reaches before any sign-in,
# and until 2026-10-03 it was handed the whole of secrets.env (infra-5).
#
# ## The TLS switch
#
# One variable, NOCTORNAL_TLS_MODE, and it is always a single token so the
# `tls` directive is never left without an argument:
#
#   NOCTORNAL_TLS_MODE=internal          Caddy's own CA. Certificates are
#                                        not publicly trusted, so a browser
#                                        warns and `curl` needs -k. This is
#                                        for a local verification run
#                                        against hostnames in /etc/hosts,
#                                        and it is the DEFAULT below --
#                                        forgetting the variable produces a
#                                        working stack with an untrusted
#                                        certificate rather than a stack
#                                        that hammers Let's Encrypt for a
#                                        hostname that does not resolve.
#
#   NOCTORNAL_TLS_MODE=ops@example.org   ACME. The value is the account
#                                        e-mail Let's Encrypt uses for
#                                        expiry notices. Both hostnames
#                                        must already resolve to this host
#                                        on the public internet and reach
#                                        port 80, or issuance fails.
#
# Issued certificates and the ACME account key live in the caddy-data
# volume. Destroying it means re-issuing, and issuance is rate-limited per
# hostname per week.
#
# ## X-Forwarded-For
#
# Caddy APPENDS the address it actually saw to any X-Forwarded-For the
# client sent. That is what makes the header trustworthy from the right and
# untrustworthy from the left, and it is why the application must be told
# NOCTORNAL_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1: with one hop it reads the LAST entry --
# Caddy's own observation -- and a client that invents earlier entries only
# lengthens a list nothing reads. Set that variable to 0, or leave it
# unset, and the client's address is whatever uvicorn reports as the peer:
# compose.yml starts it with --forwarded-allow-ips for Caddy, which makes that
# the client's own, and where a process is started any other way every request
# appears to come from Caddy: the rate limiter meters the whole internet as
# one subject, the sign-in audit records the proxy as the origin of every
# login, and session IP binding pins every session to the same address. The
# readiness register reports the unset count in production (proxy_hops_declared).
#
# ## Upload sizes (2026-10-08)
#
# `request_body max_size` stands on the five routes that take a multipart
# file (an exhibit, a sample, an e-mail exhibit, a YARA rule set version, a
# hash list) and on no other. The application writes such a body to a
# temporary file before the route has looked at who sent it, and a request
# that carries any bearer value or a session cookie is read that way to the
# route's cap whether or not the credential is live (docs/17, "a junk
# credential is still read"). The caps the application declares stay the
# authority, and its own refusal names the cap; this is the ceiling in front
# of them, and the api service's /tmp (compose.yml) is the bound behind.
#
# 256MiB is the default of both NOCTORNAL_MAX_EVIDENCE_BYTES and
# NOCTORNAL_MAX_SAMPLE_BYTES, and like theirs it counts the whole body,
# framing included. A deployment that declares either above it raises this
# line with it and the size of /tmp (infra/production/README.md, Upload
# sizes). Write it as MiB: Caddy reads 256MB as 256,000,000 bytes, which is
# under the application's own 256 MiB. A body over the limit is answered
# with the problem+json the application sends, in place of Caddy's bare 413,
# which named nothing. The pattern follows the application's own routes: a
# test reads the route table and fails on a file route it does not cover
# (apps/api/tests/test_proxy_upload_limits.py).
#
# ## Compression (2026-10-08)
#
# The console, and only the console, is compressed: /ui is 2.3 MiB of
# static text that crossed the wire whole on every visit and is about 0.7 MiB
# compressed. An API answer is what the caller may read, with what they sent
# in it, which is what a compression side channel (BREACH) needs, so none
# is compressed.
#
# ## The Server header (2026-10-08)
#
# uvicorn names itself on every answer and Caddy passed it on. `header
# -Server` takes it off both hostnames (it runs after reverse_proxy has
# copied the upstream's headers), and the same line under `handle_errors`
# takes Caddy's own off the answers Caddy makes itself, a 502 while the API
# restarts among them.

# ## Transport (infra-6, 2026-10-03)
#
# The application never sends Strict-Transport-Security (http/app.py leaves
# it to the terminator) and this is the terminator, so it is sent HERE, on
# both hostnames: a first visit, or one after a browser forgot, over plain
# http could otherwise be stripped by anything on the path, which then
# proxies the console including its login form. One year, subdomains
# included, no `preload`: preload is a commitment that cannot be taken back
# and is the operator's to make. apps/api/tests/test_g48_image_context.py
# holds the header and the two settings below to this file, since it is the
# only place they can come from.
#
# TLS 1.3 only, as docs/05's hardening checklist asks. A client that cannot
# speak it cannot reach the console.
#
# HTTP/3 is off. 443/udp is not published (compose.yml), so advertising it
# in Alt-Svc sent browsers to a port nothing answers on.
#
# ## The log (2026-10-07)
#
# Caddy writes no access log here, but on an upstream failure (a 502 while
# the API restarts) it logs the request with its headers. It redacts Cookie
# and Authorization by itself, and nothing else: X-Csrf-Token (the value of
# the __Host-csrf cookie) and X-Setup-Token (the first-run door's secret)
# reached `docker logs` verbatim, measured with Caddy 2.11.4. The filter takes
# both out of every log line this process writes.
{
	servers {
		protocols h1 h2
	}
	log {
		format filter {
			wrap json
			fields {
				request>headers>X-Csrf-Token delete
				request>headers>X-Setup-Token delete
			}
		}
	}
}

{$NOCTORNAL_HOSTNAME} {
	tls {$NOCTORNAL_TLS_MODE:internal} {
		protocols tls1.3
	}
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
	header -Server

	# Only the console is compressed (see Compression above).
	@console path /ui /ui/*
	encode @console zstd gzip

	# The five routes that take a multipart file (see Upload sizes above).
	# `[^/]+` is the application's own reading of {case_id} and {ruleset_id}:
	# it routes a path whose id is not a UUID to the same handler, which reads
	# the body before it finds that out.
	@uploads path_regexp ^/api/v1/(cases/[^/]+/(evidence|deception/emails)|samples(/screening/lists|/yara/rulesets/[^/]+/versions)?)/?$
	request_body @uploads {
		max_size 256MiB
	}
	handle_errors {
		header -Server
		@too_large expression {err.status_code} == 413
		handle @too_large {
			header Content-Type application/problem+json
			respond `{"type":"about:blank","title":"Payload too large","status":413,"detail":"an upload is capped by the proxy in front of the application (request_body max_size in infra/production/Caddyfile), and this one was refused at that cap"}` 413
		}
	}

	# The API, the console at /ui and the /api/v1/live WebSocket, which
	# Caddy upgrades without any extra configuration.
	reverse_proxy api:8000
}

{$NOCTORNAL_SAMPLE_HOSTNAME} {
	tls {$NOCTORNAL_TLS_MODE:internal} {
		protocols tls1.3
	}
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
	header -Server
	handle_errors {
		header -Server
	}

	# A SEPARATE upstream, not a path on the one above. If this ever
	# pointed at api:8000 the deployment would look correct and every
	# sample download would be refused by the application itself, because
	# `origin_split()` decides from configuration and the api process is
	# configured as the application.
	reverse_proxy sample-origin:8000
}
